<Page title="About and method" description="How CricScience writes and checks its lessons: sources on every lesson, a clear line between established and debated, and a public correction process." lede="CricScience explains why cricket works the way it does — the air around the ball, the pitch under it, the body that bowls it and the technology that judges it.">
  <h2>How lessons are written</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Every lesson cites sources.</strong> Peer-reviewed research where it exists, the MCC Laws and ICC regulations for rules, and clearly labelled secondary sources otherwise.</li>
    <li><strong>Every lesson separates what is established from what is debated.</strong> Where the science is open, the lesson says so rather than picking a side.</li>
    <li><strong>Numbers are calculated or cited.</strong> Figures such as flight times and drag come from stated physics models you can rerun in the labs. We avoid unsourced statistics about individual players.</li>
    <li><strong>Every lesson shows when it was last reviewed.</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <h2>The labs</h2>
  <p>The labs are simplified models. Each states what it includes and what it leaves out. They are meant to show the shape of the physics, not to predict a particular delivery or hit.</p>
  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>If you find an error, email <a href="mailto:hello@cricscience.com?subject=Correction">hello@cricscience.com</a> with the lesson and, ideally, a source. Corrections are reviewed and the lesson's review date is updated.</p>
  <h2>Independence</h2>
  <p>CricScience is independent. It is not affiliated with the MCC, the ICC, any national board, or any equipment or technology company. Product and system names are used only to describe them.</p>
</Page>
